WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.700
A blue crab in the Chesapeake Bay is built from atoms that were somewhere else

2
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.780
before. No one has traced the atoms in one particular crab.

3
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.780
But we can trace the routes those atoms could take.

4
00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.600
How does matter keep moving through ecosystems?

5
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.680
And what happens when people change a cycle?

6
00:00:21.683 --> 00:00:25.063
Carbon is in every sugar, fat and protein in your body.

7
00:00:26.023 --> 00:00:31.283
Photosynthesis is how plants, algae and some bacteria use light energy to build

8
00:00:31.283 --> 00:00:33.423
sugar from carbon dioxide and water.

9
00:00:34.483 --> 00:00:39.483
Respiration is how cells break down sugar with oxygen and release carbon dioxide.

10
00:00:40.763 --> 00:00:42.623
Plants, animals and microbes all do it.

11
00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:44.927
Combustion is burning.

12
00:00:45.707 --> 00:00:49.667
It joins carbon with oxygen and releases carbon dioxide quickly.

13
00:00:50.727 --> 00:00:55.347
Decomposition is the breakdown of dead organisms by fungi and bacteria.

14
00:00:56.347 --> 00:00:59.287
Their respiration returns much of the carbon to the air.

15
00:01:00.207 --> 00:01:04.827
Ocean absorption is carbon dioxide dissolving into seawater at the surface.

16
00:01:05.847 --> 00:01:07.887
Carbon dioxide also comes back out.

17
00:01:08.817 --> 00:01:13.617
Water cycles too. Evaporation turns liquid water into vapor.

18
00:01:14.697 --> 00:01:17.237
Condensation turns vapor into cloud droplets.

19
00:01:18.217 --> 00:01:21.497
Precipitation returns water to the ground as rain or snow.

20
00:01:22.697 --> 00:01:26.997
Moving water carries nutrients such as nitrogen, along with sediment and germs.

21
00:01:27.837 --> 00:01:32.837
So a rainstorm in Pennsylvania can move nitrogen into the Susquehanna River, which

22
00:01:32.837 --> 00:01:33.837
flows into the Bay.

23
00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:38.267
Your body uses nitrogen to build proteins and DNA.

24
00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:41.787
Air is about 78 percent nitrogen gas.

25
00:01:42.507 --> 00:01:46.307
But the two atoms in each molecule are held by a very strong bond.

26
00:01:47.227 --> 00:01:49.147
Most organisms cannot break it.

27
00:01:49.967 --> 00:01:53.927
Nitrogen fixation is the conversion of nitrogen gas into ammonia.

28
00:01:54.887 --> 00:01:58.487
In nature, certain microbes, mostly bacteria, do most of it.

29
00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:02.847
Nitrification changes ammonia into nitrite and then nitrate.

30
00:02:03.567 --> 00:02:06.907
Assimilation is a plant taking up nitrate or ammonium.

31
00:02:07.787 --> 00:02:12.807
Ammonification is decomposers turning wastes and dead bodies back into ammonium.

32
00:02:13.647 --> 00:02:17.747
Denitrification is bacteria turning nitrate back into nitrogen gas.

33
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:20.580
So how does matter keep moving?

34
00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:22.640
Atoms are not used up.

35
00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:27.280
They move between air, water, soil and living things, and they change form.

36
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.420
People can change a cycle.

37
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.420
Burning coal, oil and natural gas adds carbon to the air.

38
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.060
That carbon was locked underground for millions of years.

39
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.400
Water carries nitrogen from fields toward the Bay.

40
00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:42.500
No cycle runs alone.
